    <p class="take">DLinear is the honest baseline for the deep models: same windows, same scaling, same loss, but only a linear map. It scored {r3('DLinear')}, clearly behind NHITS ({r3('NHITS')}) and N-BEATS ({r3('NBEATS_generic')}). Two cautions before reading that gap as “depth wins”. The paper itself says the linear models underfit on short inputs and are meant as a simple, competitive baseline with limited capacity; our 28-week window is far from its 336 steps. And the run is not a controlled comparison: DLinear's default learning rate is ten times lower with the same 500 steps, so it is also under-trained: with the learning rate set to 1e-3 it scores 1.46 on all series, which closes about two thirds of its gap to NHITS.</p>
    <p class="cap"><b>To discuss:</b> with a 25-week average on a 28-week window, how different are the trend and the window mean? Since the whole model is one 14 × 28 matrix, what does the decomposition change at all?</p>
