    <p class="take">Things worth noticing. (1) The forecast is never fed back in for horizons up to 1,024 steps: the model was trained with whole runs of patches blanked out, so at inference the empty horizon looks like one more blanked run. (2) Roles are told to the model twice, once as a learned vector added to every patch and once through the attention masks. (3) Nothing tells t0 the date or the frequency: weekly sales and 5-minute electricity load are the same kind of input. (4) On our data a patch is 32 weeks, so the 93 weeks before the test period are 2.9 patches, and the 14-week forecast is less than half of one output patch; the model forecasts 32 weeks and the last 18 are dropped.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate" id="m-timestral">
    <h3>Inside Timestral: context tokens and separate prediction tokens</h3>
    <p>Timestral (Mistral AI, October 2026) is a family of four models, 5M, 28M, 123M and 209M parameters, with one architecture and one training recipe. The report calls it a <b>causal encoder-only</b> model: the context is read left to right like TimesFM or t0, but the forecast is produced at dedicated <b>prediction tokens</b>, like Chronos-2's future tokens. Step through Timestral-209M below. Sources: <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37585">Timestral</a> (technical report, arXiv 2610.37585, 27 October 2026), the <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Timestral-209M">Timestral-209M model card</a> and released weights, and the official inference code, <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://github.com/mistralai/timestral">mistralai/timestral</a>.</p>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="tsStep"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="0">Whole network</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="1">1 · Scale</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="2">2 · Patch + embed</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="3">3 · Prediction tokens</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="4">4 · 24 blocks</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="5">5 · Quantile head</button></div></div>
    <div class="fig wide" id="figTSarch"></div>
    <p class="stepcap" id="tsCap"></p>

    <h3 class="mt-4">Decoupled Horizon Forecasting, the one new idea</h3>
    <p>TimesFM, Toto and t0 train a causal model to predict the next patch from every position. To also train forecasts several patches ahead, TiRex, Toto-2.0, TimesFM-3 and t0 blank out runs of input patches (<b>contiguous patch masking</b>). The report's objection: a patch blanked to create a long-distance forecast is also missing as context for every later forecast in the same training sequence, so “how far ahead do we train” and “how much context is hidden” are tied together. Timestral gives each forecast patch two tokens. Its <b>context token</b> holds its real values and keeps feeding later forecasts; its <b>prediction token</b> is where it is forecast. Context tokens never attend to prediction tokens, so one causal pass over the context serves every forecast block of the sequence. The price is about twice as many tokens in training (report §3.1). At inference there is one block after the context, up to 32 patches = 1,024 steps; longer horizons go block by block, feeding back the predicted medians.</p>
    <div class="tw">
      <table class="mtable">
        <thead><tr><th>Part (Timestral-209M)</th><th>Shape in the released weights</th><th class="num">Weights</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Patch embedding</td><td>residual MLP 64 → 3,072 → 768 (SiLU) with a linear skip 64 → 768; input = 32 values + 32 missing flags</td><td class="num">2,609,664</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Prediction token</td><td>one learned vector of 768, copied to every forecast patch of every variate</td><td class="num">768</td></tr>
          <tr><td>18 time-only blocks</td><td>RMSNorm, QKV 768 → 2,304, sigmoid gate 768 → 768, output 768 → 768 (12 heads of 64, no biases), then RMSNorm and SwiGLU 768 → 2 × 2,048 → 768</td><td class="num">18 × 7,669,248</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6 time + variate blocks (4th, 8th, … 24th)</td><td>the same, with a second gated attention across variates between the time attention and the feed-forward</td><td class="num">6 × 10,619,136</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Final RMSNorm</td><td>768</td><td class="num">768</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quantile head</td><td>residual MLP 768 → 3,072 → 672 with a linear skip, no biases; 672 = 32 steps × 21 quantiles</td><td class="num">4,939,776</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td>203 tensors, float32, 837 MB file</td><td class="num"><b>209,312,256</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap">Counted from the header of the released <code>model.safetensors</code>; it equals the report's 209.3M (Table 1: d<sub>model</sub> 768, 24 blocks, 12 heads, SwiGLU width 2,048, head dimension 64, variate attention in every fourth block). The other sizes: 5M (192 wide, 8 blocks), 28M (384, 12), 123M (640, 20).</p>

    <h3 class="mt-4">What is stated, what we checked, and what is not published</h3>
    <div class="tw">
      <table class="mtable">
        <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sizes, block schedule, gated attention, RoPE on half of each head, 21 quantiles, patch 32, context up to 32,768 steps</td><td><b>stated</b> in the report (§3.3, Table 1) and model card, <b>checked</b> in the 209M <code>config.json</code> and weight shapes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Two token streams and their attention rules, roles only in the masks, covariates never read targets</td><td><b>stated</b> (§3.1, §3.2) and <b>checked</b> in <code>masks.py</code> of the inference code</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Embedding and head MLPs use SiLU and a hidden width of 4 × 768; quantile increments are scaled by the gap between neighbouring levels (factor 3.0 in the 209M config)</td><td><b>from the code only</b>: the report just says “two-layer residual MLP” and “cumulative sums of positive (softplus) increments”</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The released forecaster averages each forecast with the mirrored forecast of the negated series (two passes)</td><td><b>checked</b>: on by default in <code>forecaster.py</code>; the report mentions it in its speed tests (§4.3)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pretrained only on synthetic series (8,192-step context), then post-trained on real + synthetic (32,768 steps); pinball loss over 21 levels, equal weights</td><td><b>stated</b> (§4.1). The generators, data sizes, step counts and the block-length distribution are <b>not published</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Licence and benchmark scores</td><td>a modified MIT licence: no rights for companies with more than $20 million monthly revenue without a commercial licence from Mistral (model card <code>LICENSE</code>). Scores are self-reported</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="take">Things worth noticing. (1) Timestral adds <b>no</b> role embedding: a target and a covariate get the same kind of token, and only the masks and the normalisation tell them apart. t0 does both. (2) The prediction token at patch j forecasts patch j itself; in t0 the token at patch j forecasts patch j + 1. (3) Each forecast patch is unscaled with statistics that include the earlier patches of the same block, which are not known at inference, so the code plugs in the predicted medians there; training uses the same rule, so the two match (§3.3). (4) The report notes the model needs no GPU: 113 ms for one 96-step forecast from 2,048 steps of context on 8 CPU threads.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate" id="m-fm-masks">
    <h3>Who can see what: the attention masks of t0-gamma and Timestral</h3>
    <p>Both models forecast with known-future covariates without ever letting the target's own future leak in, and both do it with attention masks, not with separate networks. The toy series below has one target <b>y</b>, one past-only covariate <b>x</b> (say, competitor price) and one known-future covariate <b>z</b> (say, a planned promotion), six context patches and three forecast patches. Pick a model and an attention type, then click any token to see which tokens it may read. The rules are copied from the two inference codes (<code>t0/mask.py</code> and <code>timestral/masks.py</code>).</p>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="fmModel"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="t0">t0-gamma</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="ts">Timestral</button></div><div class="seg" id="fmLayer"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="time">time attention</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="var">variate attention</button></div></div>
    <div class="fig wide" id="figFMmask"></div>
    <div class="readout" id="fmOut"></div>
    <div class="fig wide" id="figFMlayers"></div>
    <p class="cap"><b>How to read this strip.</b> Each box is one layer of the network. The data flows through them from left to right, layer 1 first and layer 24 last, and every layer slightly rewrites each token. A layer has one of two jobs:</p>
    <ul class="cap list-disc pl-6">
      <li><b>T (time attention):</b> each series looks back along its own history. A token reads earlier patches of the same row: the target reads its own past, a covariate reads its own past.</li>
      <li><b>V (variate attention):</b> the series look at each other at the same moment. At one patch position, the target reads the covariates (and, depending on the rules above, they read it).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cap">Each attention step is followed by a small feed-forward network applied to every token on its own. <b>t0-gamma</b> uses separate layers in the pattern T, T, V, repeated 8 times: 16 time layers and 8 variate layers. <b>Timestral</b> has a time attention in all 24 layers. In every 4th layer (layers 4, 8, …, 24) a variate attention is added inside the same layer, which is why those boxes are split in two. Inside those layers, time attention runs first and variate attention second, and then the layer's one feed-forward network runs (checked in <code>timestral/layers.py</code> and the 209M <code>block_schedule</code>). So both models alternate “look along time” with “look across series”; t0 mixes across series more often (8 times versus 6).</p>
    <div class="tw">
      <table class="mtable">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>t0-gamma</th><th>Timestral-209M</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Size</td><td>255.6M, width 1,024</td><td>209.3M, width 768 (also 5M, 28M, 123M)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forecast tokens</td><td>the horizon is appended as blanked patches in the same rows; each token forecasts the next patch</td><td>separate prediction tokens (one learned vector at each forecast position); each forecasts its own patch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Layer pattern</td><td>24 layers: time, time, variate, repeated 8 times</td><td>24 blocks of time attention; every 4th also has variate attention (6)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Patch input</td><td>values, within-patch time index, validity mask (96)</td><td>values, missing flags (64)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roles (target, past, known-future)</td><td>learned role embedding + masks</td><td>masks only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Past covariate ↔ target</td><td>read each other</td><td>target reads covariate, covariate does not read target</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Attention details</td><td>QK-norm; RoPE with a decay for distant patches</td><td>sigmoid output gate; RoPE on half of each head</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scaling</td><td>running mean and std up to each step, then arcsinh</td><td>running mean and std up to the end of each patch, then arcsinh; binary known-future covariates unscaled</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quantiles</td><td>21; the lowest predicted, each next one adds a softplus step</td><td>21; the median predicted, the others fan out by softplus steps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Training data</td><td>t0-alpha: GIFT-Eval pretrain + 4 synthetic generator families; gamma not published</td><td>synthetic only, then real + synthetic with longer context</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Licence</td><td>Apache-2.0</td><td>modified MIT (revenue cap)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="take">The shared trick is the “two-hop leak”. A known-future covariate z sees its whole row, including the forecast weeks. If z could also read the target at week 10, a later time layer could carry that value back to z at week 8, and a later variate layer could hand it to the target at week 8, before week 10 has happened. Both models close the leak the same way: <b>z may only read other z</b>. Timestral goes one step further and also stops past covariates from reading the target, treating every covariate as exogenous. Chronos-2 does not need the rule: it trains one forecast origin per example, reads the context in both directions, and never has the target's future values in its input (Timestral report §2.2).</p>
    <p class="cap"><b>To discuss:</b> in Timestral, how does a promotion planned for forecast week 3 reach the target's prediction token, given that z's prediction tokens only read z's context before the forecast block? (Hint: z's context tokens see the whole row.) Why might a 32-week patch be a poor fit for 14-week forecasts of weekly retail sales, and what would you check before trusting either model on our items?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="plate" id="m-fm-compare">
    <h3>The four foundation models side by side</h3>
    <p>Chronos-2 and TimesFM 2.5 are the two foundation models in Lab 04; t0-gamma and Timestral are explained above. All four cut a series into patches and run a transformer over them, and all four can forecast a series they have never seen. They differ in four ways: which direction time attention runs, how several series are combined, where covariates enter, and what they were trained on. Every row below was checked against the model's own paper or technical report, and against its official code or released configuration where the paper is silent or describes an older version. Sources: Ansari et al., <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15821">Chronos-2: From Univariate to Universal Forecasting</a> (2025) and the <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://huggingface.co/amazon/chronos-2">chronos-2 config</a>; Das, Kong, Sen and Zhou, <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10688">A decoder-only foundation model for time-series forecasting</a> (ICML 2024, which describes TimesFM 1.0), with the <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://huggingface.co/google/timesfm-2.5-200m-pytorch">TimesFM 2.5 model card</a> and <a class="text-primary-600 hover:underline" href="https://github.com/google-research/timesfm">code</a> for 2.5, which has no paper of its own; the t0 and Timestral reports linked above.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-4">How one forecasting task enters each model</h3>
    <p>Pick a model and a task. Each box is one patch-token of one variate; the dashed line is the forecast origin. The text underneath says what the model does with each row.</p>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="fcModel"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="ch">Chronos-2</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="tf">TimesFM 2.5</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="t0">t0-gamma</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="ts">Timestral</button></div><div class="seg" id="fcTask"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="uni">one series</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="multi">3 target variates</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="cov">target + covariates</button></div></div>
    <div class="fig wide" id="figFMtask"></div>
    <div class="readout" id="fcOut"></div>

    <h3 class="mt-4">The comparison</h3>
    <div class="tw">
      <table class="mtable">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Chronos-2</th><th>TimesFM 2.5</th><th>t0-gamma</th><th>Timestral-209M</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th colspan="5">Who and what</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Made by, released</td><td>Amazon, Oct 2025</td><td>Google, Sep 2025 (covariates added Oct 2025)</td><td>The Forecasting Company, Oct 2026</td><td>Mistral AI, Oct 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Size</td><td>120M (a 28M version too)</td><td>200M, plus an optional 30M quantile head</td><td>256M</td><td>209M (also 5M, 28M, 123M)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Weights licence</td><td>Apache-2.0</td><td>Apache-2.0</td><td>Apache-2.0</td><td>modified MIT, no use above $20M monthly company revenue</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Design idea, in the authors' words</td><td>“in-context learning” across a <i>group</i> of related series, variates or covariates, learned from synthetic multivariate data</td><td>a decoder-only model trained “similar to LLMs”: predict the next patch from every prefix of the series</td><td>“forecasting with context”: targets, past and known-future covariates in one causal model</td><td>an approximation of Bayesian posterior inference (the prior-data fitted network view); pretrain on synthetic data only</td></tr>

          <tr><th colspan="5">Structure</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Type</td><td>encoder-only (T5 encoder); attention in both directions over the whole context</td><td>decoder-only; causal attention</td><td>decoder-style; causal attention (known-future rows both ways)</td><td>causal encoder plus a separate stream of prediction tokens</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Layers</td><td>12 blocks, each with time attention and group attention; width 768, 12 heads, ReLU feed-forward 3,072</td><td>20 layers, time attention only; width 1,280, 16 heads, swish feed-forward 1,280, QK-norm, RoPE</td><td>24 layers: time, time, variate, × 8; width 1,024, 8 heads, SwiGLU, QK-norm, RoPE with distance decay</td><td>24 blocks of time attention, variate attention added in every 4th; width 768, 12 heads, SwiGLU, sigmoid-gated attention</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Patch in / out</td><td>16 / 16 steps</td><td>32 in; 128 out per decoding step (point and 9 quantiles); the optional quantile head covers up to 1,024 steps</td><td>32 / 32</td><td>32 / 32</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where the forecast is read</td><td>from tokens of the future patches, placed after a REG separator token</td><td>from the last context token, which predicts the next 128 steps; longer horizons decode autoregressively</td><td>from blanked horizon patches: the token at patch p predicts patch p + 1</td><td>from the prediction token at patch j, which predicts patch j</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Horizon in one pass</td><td>up to 64 patches = 1,024 steps</td><td>128 steps per decoding step; the optional quantile head gives quantiles up to 1,024 steps</td><td>1,024 steps</td><td>1,024 steps</td></tr>

          <tr><th colspan="5">How the data is handled</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Scaling</td><td>each variate standardised with the mean and standard deviation of its whole context, then asinh; known-future values use the same context statistics</td><td>running mean and standard deviation, updated patch by patch, applied per patch (2.5 code; the 1.0 paper scaled by the first patch); no asinh. Forecasts are guaranteed to move with shifts and positive rescalings of the input</td><td>running mean and standard deviation up to each step, then arcsinh; known-future covariates over the whole window</td><td>running mean and standard deviation up to the end of each patch, then asinh; known-future covariates over the whole window, 0/1 ones left as they are</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Missing values</td><td>mask channel; values set to 0</td><td>padding mask fed with the values</td><td>validity channel</td><td>missing-flag channel</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Time and frequency information</td><td>a relative time index per step (−T/C … (H−1)/C) as an input channel</td><td>none (2.5 dropped the frequency indicator of 2.0); RoPE positions only</td><td>a within-patch time index; RoPE patch positions</td><td>none; RoPE patch positions only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Context length</td><td>8,192 (trained at 2,048, then extended)</td><td>up to 16,384</td><td>not stated for gamma; t0-alpha trained on windows up to 8,192</td><td>32,768 (pretrained at 8,192, post-trained at 32,768)</td></tr>

          <tr><th colspan="5">Several series at once</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Multivariate targets</td><td>give the variates the same group id; group attention mixes them at each patch position in every block, with no positional encoding</td><td>not in the network: each variate is forecast as its own series (native multivariate support arrived in TimesFM 3.0, under a non-commercial weights licence)</td><td>variates of one series share a group id; variate attention in every third layer</td><td>variates of one series share a group id; variate attention in every fourth block, among context tokens and among prediction tokens</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unrelated series in one batch</td><td>can be put in one group on purpose (“cross learning”), e.g. for short or cold-start series</td><td>always independent</td><td>independent unless they share a group id</td><td>independent unless they share a group id</td></tr>

          <tr><th colspan="5">Exogenous variables</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Past-only covariates</td><td>extra rows in the group; their future patches are masked</td><td>not supported (XReg needs covariate values over the horizon)</td><td>extra rows; horizon blanked; target and covariate read each other</td><td>extra rows; only prediction tokens over the horizon; they read other covariates, never the target</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Known-future covariates</td><td>extra rows whose future patches hold the planned values; reach the target through group attention</td><td><b>outside the network</b>: “XReg” fits a ridge linear regression of the target on the covariates over the context; by default the regression goes first and TimesFM forecasts what it leaves over</td><td>extra rows, future values filled in; time attention both ways; they read only other known-future rows</td><td>extra rows, future values filled in; time attention both ways; they read only other known-future rows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Categorical covariates</td><td>target encoding (one target) or ordinal codes (several targets)</td><td>one-hot, inside XReg</td><td>not documented</td><td>target encoding (model card)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Static covariates</td><td>not covered in the report</td><td>numeric and categorical, inside XReg</td><td>“left to future work” (t0 report §2)</td><td>not covered in the report</td></tr>

          <tr><th colspan="5">Output and training</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Output</td><td>21 quantiles, 0.01 to 0.99</td><td>mean + 9 quantiles, 0.1 to 0.9</td><td>21 quantiles</td><td>21 quantiles</td></tr>
