    <p class="take">What this means for weekly retail sales like ours. (1) <b>Covariates:</b> only Chronos-2, t0 and Timestral put price and promotions inside the network, where attention can learn how they interact with the history; TimesFM 2.5 adds a linear regression on top, so a promotion can only add a linear effect fitted on the history. Lab 04's biggest gain came from giving Chronos-2 the covariates. (2) <b>Short history:</b> 93 weeks is just under 6 patches for Chronos-2 but under 3 for the other three, which use 32-step patches. (3) <b>Several items at once:</b> only Chronos-2 is designed to pool unrelated items of a batch in context; the other three treat each series on its own unless its rows share a group id. (4) <b>Licences:</b> three are Apache-2.0; Timestral's weights cannot be used by larger companies without a commercial licence.</p>
    <p class="cap">Where the paper describes an older version (TimesFM 1.0, t0-alpha), the table uses the newer version's code or configuration and says so. “Not published” means we found no primary source; we did not fill those cells from blog posts or third-party write-ups.</p>
  </div>
